+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to your morning</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-intro">Good coffee begins long before the first roast. A closer look at the people, places, and decisions behind every bean we bring home.</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-tools">
+        <button class="btn" id="summarize-journal" type="button" aria-controls="journal-summary">Summarize</button>
+        <p>A few key points, made with your browser’s built-in AI.</p>
+      </div>
+      <p class="journal-status" id="journal-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
+      <div class="journal-summary" id="journal-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title" hidden>
+        <h3 id="summary-title">The sourcing story, in brief</h3>
+        <ul id="journal-summary-points"></ul>
+      </div>
+
+      <article class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>Every coffee has a story before it reaches our roastery. It begins with a particular hillside, a season of weather, and a grower making hundreds of small decisions about the land. Our sourcing process starts by listening to that story. We look for producers and sourcing partners who can tell us where a coffee was grown, how it was harvested, and what went into preparing it for export. A delicious sample is the beginning of a conversation, never the whole conversation.</p>
+
+        <p>We seek out relationships built around care for both the crop and the people growing it. When we evaluate a potential coffee, we ask our partners about farm practices, working conditions, and how prices are agreed. We value clear records and honest conversations over a reassuring label alone. Coffee is a seasonal agricultural product, and the costs of producing it change. Our aim is to choose partners who recognize that quality depends on growers having the resources to plan for another harvest, care for their trees, and pay for the work each season requires.</p>
+
+        <p>Then comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and taste them side by side, paying attention to sweetness, balance, clarity, and finish. We want to understand what makes each lot distinctive rather than look for the same flavor everywhere. Altitude, variety, soil, and processing all play a part: a washed coffee might offer a crisp, delicate cup, while a naturally processed lot can bring a rounder texture and ripe fruit notes. We taste again as the coffee cools, take detailed notes, and consider how it might feel in your everyday brew.</p>
+
+        <p>Choosing a sample is only one step. Before a coffee joins our lineup, we work with our sourcing partners to understand its journey through milling, storage, and shipping. Careful drying and protection from moisture help preserve the character we tasted at origin. When the beans arrive, we sample them again and compare them with our earlier notes. If something has changed, we investigate before deciding how to roast or serve that lot. Traceability matters here because it lets us connect what is in the cup with the choices made along the way.</p>
+
+        <p>At the roastery, our job is to bring that work into focus. We develop a roast profile for each coffee in small batches, adjusting time and heat to find a balance that lets its natural sweetness and character come through. We taste our production roasts regularly and refine our approach as the beans settle and age. Some coffees become a comforting foundation for espresso; others shine as a seasonal filter offering. The goal is always a cup that honors the ingredient and feels good to return to.</p>
+
+        <p>Our menu changes because harvests change. Buying seasonally gives us room to celebrate fresh arrivals and to keep learning from the people behind them. We see sourcing as an ongoing practice of asking better questions, tasting carefully, and making thoughtful choices. By the time you open a bag or take that first sip, many hands have contributed to it. We want the care behind those hands to be something you can taste.</p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
